INT. LAUNDROMAT - NIGHT

Dryers turning. Fluorescent hum. A broken vending machine.

Five Hargrove-Delgados. One bench.

GRANDMA CONCEPCIÓN, eighties, folds towels. Slow.

Her daughter, MARISOL, fifties, scrolls her phone. Doesn't read it.

MARISOL's husband, DESMOND, sixties, reads a newspaper upside down. Maybe on purpose.

Their son, JUNIOR, twenty, headphones. Elsewhere.

Their niece, TAMSIN, nine, counts quarters. Arranges them. Rearranges them.

CONCEPCIÓN
(to everyone)
Almost done. Nobody move.

JUNIOR
Nobody's moving, Abuela.

MARISOL
Junior. Take those off.

He doesn't. She sighs and goes back to her phone.

DESMOND
(muttering)
Forty minutes for two towels.

TAMSIN
Uncle Des, you're holding it wrong.

DESMOND
It's fine.

She takes it from him, turns it right side up. He nods, gravely.

CONCEPCIÓN
Tamsin, mija, how many?

TAMSIN
Eleven.

CONCEPCIÓN
We need twelve.

A pause. They all look at the machine. It hums back.

MARISOL
(to no one)
We always need twelve.
